ent settings  people do different kinds of work  and there's no federal  standard occupational classification for  informatics we were hoping there would  be but they chose not to add one in the  most recent update  maybe they will in the future but the  the there's been a substantial growth  and  anywhere um and this growth is estimated  to continue anywhere from nineteen  thousand to a hundred and fifty  000 jobs on top of the 160 000 that  already exists  it's a paper i published a couple years  ago  in terms of numbers the largest  opportunity is in health care settings  but there's plenty of other  opportunities for those who  don't want to work in health care  settings in  research settings research institutes  academic  centers industry many of our graduates  go work  in industry and in  government this was a report  published in 2018 by  the um i have to remember  it's some entity connected with the  united nations i  apologize um  they looked at um the workforce in 2018  and 2022  and which areas were emerging and  declining  and if you look in the emerging areas  you see things that are related to  biomedical informatics  data analysts and scientists biologists  and geneticists ai and machine learning  informational technology information  technology environment  big data um specialist medical  practitioners to some sense  and the things that are declining like  data entry clerks  um automate a factory workers  um things like that so  there are growth opportunities  in um biomedical informatics  in terms of careers this is a busy slide  and um but i just want to kind of show  one of the challenges we have in  biomedical informatics  is the different areas within  informatics  so you may hear the term bioinformatics  i'm looking in the middle part of the  slide  that is informatics that's mostly  focused on cellular molecular  levels moving to the person  medical or clinical informatics or to  the population public health informatics  and people sometimes c