ts from different backgrounds  health professions  life sciences computer science and  related technology disciplines health  information management even other  majors business majors and so forth they  all  they come in those yellow arrows then  they all get mixed in  with the red in our graduate program  and then they go out and take different  kinds of jobs and oftentimes what you do  when you get out  is partially dependent on what you do  did when you entered a classic example  of this  is the position of chief medical  information officer  which many hospitals have one or  sometimes more than one  um that's typically a physician or  someone who's  trained in healthcare practice  other people who may take more technical  jobs  come in with more technical backgrounds  like computer science or  other i.t related areas  in the end though so there's no single  career pathway or ladder  but in the end people find jobs in  healthcare systems in research settings  in industry and academia  a few other programs of note we also  offer what we call  fellowship programs these are  funded programs so people who are  fellows get a stipend  and their tuition covered and it's  mostly funded through our national  library medicine training grant  though also for physicians in who are  pursuing  the physician subspecialty of clinical  informatics  we have a second fellowship program that  they can enter  and i've mentioned 10 by 10 already  which is a continuing education course  we offer in  partnership with the american medical  informatics association  and about 10 to 15  of those students go on who complete 10  by 10  go on to graduate study and nearly 2 700  have completed that course since 2005.  so we think our program provides  value we are cognizant that  higher education is expensive we  strive to keep our tuition and fees  comparable to other programs  and then we like to add value on top of  that so our cutting edge curriculum  that's based on a solid foundation our 